{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_15-11-32-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run; python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('state.json'))\ncurr={e['id']:e for e in st['interpretations']}\nit=None\nfor e in st['interpretations']:\n    if e['id']=='6a04a87566ed4823a154a1f58d96d9f1-interpretation-0': it=e\npar=(\\\" The execution side of that decision is pinned to source as well. An instance that is COMMITTED with a non-nil zero-length command list passes every guard on the \\\"\n \\\"executor's path: executeCommands leaves it alone rather than routing it to recovery because its status is at least COMMITTED and its Cmds are non-nil (epaxos/epaxos.go:398-404), \\\"\n \\\"executeCommand admits it once its status is COMMITTED (epaxos/exec.go:24-30), and strongconnect refuses only a nil slice (epaxos/exec.go:76-78). The loop that applies a batch is \\\"\n \\\"bounded by len(w.Cmds) (epaxos/exec.go:122-127), so a zero-length batch applies nothing, writes no reply even where the instance still holds pending proposals, and the instance is \\\"\n \\\"nevertheless marked EXECUTED at the end of its SCC block (epaxos/exec.go:132); executeCommands then advances ExecedUpTo over it (epaxos/epaxos.go:398-402 and 421-423), so the \\\"\n \\\"instance neither stalls its row nor comes back through recovery, and the instances queued behind it become executable. The client-visible effect therefore has two independent \\\"\n \\\"causes: the reply write sits inside the per-command loop, which never runs for a zero-length batch, and the re-offer guard is not satisfied because handleCommit requires exactly \\\"\n \\\"one command with operation NONE.\\\")\nlim=[]\nfor x in it['limitations']:\n    if x.startswith(\\\"The empty-command decision's execution-side effects\\\"):\n        lim.append(\\\"The empty-command decision's execution-side effects are now read off the executor's own guards and loops with their anchors (epaxos/exec.go:24-30, 76-78, 122-134; epaxos/epaxos.go:398-423) rather than inferred loosely, but they are still not executed: no run drove the executor, so the ordering of dependents and any state-machine effect of a zero-length batch under a real run remain unexercised. The client-side effect is measured for this scenario, including the absence of a re-offer and the empty reply writer.\\\")\n    else:\n        lim.append(x)\nce=list(it['counterevidence'])+[\\\"The executor reading is a source derivation: the guards and loops cited are the ones a run would take, but no receipt shows an instance with a zero-length batch actually reaching EXECUTED.\\\"]\nout={\\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"Extended the empty-command account to the executor, which the recorded limitation had left as a loose consequence: the batch is applied by a loop bounded by len(w.Cmds), so a COMMITTED instance with a non-nil zero-length command list applies nothing, writes no reply, is still marked EXECUTED, and has ExecedUpTo advanced over it, which also keeps it out of the recovery path. The anchors for each guard and loop are named, the two independent causes of the missing client reply are separated, and the limitation and counterevidence now say plainly that this is a source derivation rather than an executed one. No measurement, statement of the other entries or citation changes.\\\"),\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[{\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"0757162a4aa64ef4a58a90002641eb27-question-0\\\",\\\"experiment_id\\\":it['experiment_id'],\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":it['source_ids'],\\\"conclusion\\\":it['conclusion'],\\\"defect_claim\\\":it['defect_claim'],\n  \\\"executions\\\":it['executions'],\\\"rationale\\\":it['rationale']+par,\\\"counterevidence\\\":ce,\\\"limitations\\\":lim,\n  \\\"joint_history\\\":it['joint_history'],\\\"answers\\\":it['answers']}]}\njson.dump(out,open('draft/update48.json','w'),ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)\nprint('wrote')\nPY\ncd draft; /ho